# kaniko and docker read .dockerignore from the build context root, and the
# backend is its own context (the Jenkinsfile's
# helmCharts.kaniko("backend/Dockerfile", "backend", ...) call). A .dockerignore
# at the repository root is never read for this build.
#
# Everything listed here is a path the Dockerfile does not COPY — it takes only
# pyproject.toml, poetry.lock, the root *.py files and app/ — so this file
# cannot change what CI builds. It only stops the dev environment from
# streaming its tool output to the builder: the lint and test gates leave
# .mypy_cache, .pytest_cache and .ruff_cache behind, and CI never noticed
# because it builds from a clean clone.
.git
.venv
**/__pycache__
.mypy_cache
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
.env
.env.*
tests
docs
.claude
logs
tmp
